{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs realized levels, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (≈1–3 €/t on average; larger when the market is falling).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model reacts too slowly to accelerating sell-offs and subsequent rebounds—overpredicts early in declines and undercaptures the depth. Increase sensitivity to recent momentum (e.g., weight last 5–10 days more) and allow faster slope changes.",
    "Overshoot in mid-horizon: around days ~10–20 the forecast often implies a rebound/plateau that does not materialize; realized prices keep drifting lower. Damp mid-horizon mean-reversion strength (reduce bounce amplitude).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon; near-term (1–5d) bias is small, but 10–30d forecasts are increasingly high. Apply a horizon-scaled downward correction that increases with lead time.",
    "Downtrend regime adjustment: when the last 2–3 weeks show a clear lower-high/lower-low pattern, cap any forecasted rallies and enforce continued drift lower unless there is a strong recent reversal signal."
  ]
}
